      "id": "DC_kwDOKrJqmc4BF2hU",
      "body": "I had AI configure a raspberry pi3 like so:\r\n\r\n> the Orbic connects to it over USB as a private network (orbic0, 192.168.3.2 ↔ 192.168.3.1) with no default route, so the modem never carries the Pi's traffic. On it, a Python bridge does two things — it polls Rayhunter's HTTP API every 30 seconds for health the device can't report about itself (daemon dead, capture stalled, disk filling, battery/unplugged), and it runs a small HTTP listener impersonating an ntfy server so Rayhunter pushes cell-site warnings to it the instant a heuristic fires. Both feed MQTT on your Home Assistant broker, where 19 auto-discovered entities appear as the device \"Rayhunter (Orbic)\". Alongside that, an rclone WebDAV server gives Rayhunter somewhere to upload finished recordings over the USB link — archived to /var/lib/rayhunter-archive, never over cellular. Claude Opus 5\r\n\r\n<img width=\"431\" height=\"531\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e6f88763-d04b-4272-a064-618ec71c9d60\" />\r\n",
      "author": {
        "login": "andylittle",
        "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12177246?u=340ff8b29dc4f53dca4a9444c862670d4f133efb&v=4",
        "url": "https://github.com/andylittle"
      },
